---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeFancyText from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeFancyText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="fancy-text-generator">
  <DevEncodeFancyText client:load sample="fancy text" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>These are characters, not fonts</h2>
    <p>
      The fancy text generator does not change the font. Each style replaces your letters with different
      Unicode characters that are drawn in a particular way. <code>𝐀</code> is not a bold <code>A</code>;
      it is U+1D400, MATHEMATICAL BOLD CAPITAL A, a separate character.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the style is in the characters, it stays when you paste into places with no formatting: an
      Instagram bio, a LinkedIn headline, a Discord nickname, a form field, a spreadsheet cell.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the characters come from</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Unicode block</th><th>Gives you</th><th>Reliability</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Mathematical Alphanumeric Symbols</td><td>Bold, italic, script, fraktur, double-struck, monospace</td><td>Very good: designed for maths, borrowed for style</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Halfwidth and Fullwidth Forms</td><td>Ｆｕｌｌｗｉｄｔｈ vaporwave text</td><td>Excellent: all CJK-capable fonts have it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Enclosed Alphanumerics</td><td>Ⓑⓤⓑⓑⓛⓔ and 🄱🄾🅇🄴🄳 letters</td><td>Mixed on older Android</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Phonetic Extensions</td><td>Sᴍᴀʟʟ ᴄᴀᴘs, superscript, subscript</td><td>Good, with a few gaps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Combining Diacritical Marks</td><td>Strikethrough, underline, cursed text</td><td>Renders everywhere, positions vary</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Where it works and where it breaks</h2>
    <p>
      Bold, italic, script, monospace and fullwidth are safe on iOS, Android, Windows, macOS and all major
      social platforms. Support for the boxed sets (circled, squared, negative) is less complete, mostly on
      older Android devices and in some desktop apps that fall back to a limited system font. The
      regional-indicator style shows as flag-style tiles on phones and as plain letters on many desktops.
    </p>
    <p>
      You cannot test this on your own device: you see your own font stack, not that of your reader. When it
      matters, send the text to yourself on a different phone first.
    </p>

    <h2>The costs, stated plainly</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Accessibility: screen readers often read mathematical letters one at a time, as &ldquo;mathematical
        bold capital A&rdquo;, or skip them. Never style text that carries information someone needs.
      </li>
      <li>
        Searchability: styled characters are different bytes, so a search for the plain word does not find
        them. Keep your real name and keywords in ordinary text.
      </li>
      <li>
        Copy fidelity: some systems normalise or remove these characters, including a few databases, most
        username fields, and many form validators.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Copying on mobile</h2>
    <p>
      Each style has its own copy button that writes directly to the clipboard, with a fallback for browsers
      that block the modern clipboard API. Without a button, you must select text by hand, which on a phone
      means a long-press and a drag, and you can easily copy the label with the sample.
    </p>

    <h2>Everything runs in this tab</h2>
    <p>
      The transformations are simple codepoint arithmetic in your browser as you type. Nothing you enter is
      sent, which matters because people often use these styles on names, handles and private messages.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
